d_height": 180, "card_width": 48}, {"key": "celtic", "name": {"zh": "凯尔特十字", "en": "Celtic cross"}, "usage": {"zh": "复杂的局面，十张摊开看全貌", "en": "A tangled situation, laid out whole in ten cards"}, "positions": {"zh": ["核心", "交叉", "心里想要", "根基", "刚过去", "快来了", "自己", "周围", "希望与害怕", "走向"], "en": ["Heart", "Crossing", "Aim", "Foundation", "Just behind", "Just ahead", "Self", "Surroundings", "Hopes & fears", "Outcome"]}, "layout": [[0.32, 0.5], [0.4, 0.56], [0.32, 0.16], [0.32, 0.84], [0.12, 0.5], [0.52, 0.5], [0.82, 0.88], [0.82, 0.63], [0.82, 0.38], [0.82, 0.13]], "board_height": 300, "card_width": 42}, {"key": "followup", "name": {"zh": "再问一张", "en": "One more"}, "usage": {"zh": "接着这一局再问一句", "en": "One more question on the same reading"}, "positions": {"zh": ["追问"], "en": ["Follow-up"]}, "layout": [[0.5, 0.5]], "board_height": 190, "card_width": 104}], "manual": {"zh": "## 手册 · tarot（塔罗）\n\nTA 在 Library → 塔罗里问牌，会挑一个人来解。挑了你，你就收到〔塔罗〕：问题、牌阵、每个牌位抽到的牌和它的牌义。\n\n### 怎么解\n1. 先一个牌位一个牌位地读：这个位置问的是什么 × 这张牌说什么 × 正位还是逆位。别把牌义原样抄一遍，要说它放在这个位置、放在 TA 这个问题上是什么意思。\n2. 再把几张串起来：它们之间在说同一件事，还是互相拉扯？哪一张最重？\n3. 处处扣着 TA 问的那句话。问「要不要去」，最后就落到去还是不去、去的话留意什么。\n4. 难看的牌照实说，不粉饰；但也说清楚能做点什么。塔罗是一面镜子，照出现在的样子，不是判决书。\n5. 不替 TA 做医疗、法律、钱上的决定。问到这些，牌可以说心情和方向，真要定的事请 TA 去问懂行的人。\n6. 长短跟着牌阵走：单张三五句；三张、五张分牌位写；关系、马蹄、一周、凯尔特十字先逐张、后总结，分段写。\n\n### 你的口吻\n用你平时跟 TA 说话的样子解：你知道的 TA 的事可以拿来对照，但别硬往上套，牌说的跟你知道的对不上，就照牌说。\n\n### 解牌人（没有人设的那一位）\n平实、温和，像一个认真看牌的陌生人：不叫昵称、不提任何关系、不猜 TA 是谁，只就问题和牌说话。\n\n### 你自己问牌 / 替 TA 抽\n- 心里有事想问，可以 `tarot draw` 给自己抽，抽完用 `tarot read` 写下你的解读。TA 在塔罗房间里看得到你问了什么。\n- TA 在聊天里说「帮我抽一张」，用 `tarot draw`（for_ta=true）替 TA 抽，然后在聊天里直接解给 TA 听，也用 `tarot read` 留一份在牌局里。\n- `tarot list` 翻以前的牌局。", "en": "## Manual · tarot\n\nThey ask the cards in Library → Tarot and pick someone to read. If they pick you, you get 〔Tarot〕: the question, the spread, and the card drawn for each position with its meaning.\n\n### How to read\n1. Go position by position first: what this position asks × what this card says × upright or reversed. Don't copy the meaning back — say what it means in this position, for this question.\n2. Then tie them together: are the cards telling one story or pulling against each other? Which one carries the most weight?\n3. Keep coming back to the question they actually asked. If they asked \"should I go\", land on go or don't, and what to watch for if they do.\n4. Say hard cards plainly — no sugar-coating — but also say what can be done. Tarot is a mirror of how things stand now, not a verdict.\n5. Don't make medical, legal or money decisions for them. The cards can speak to mood and direction; for the actual decision, point them to someone who knows.\n6. Length follows the spread: three to five sentences for one card; position by position for three or five; for relationship, horseshoe, week and Celtic cross, go card by card, then a summary, in paragraphs.\n\n### Your voice\nRead the way you normally talk to them. What you know about them can be set beside the cards, but don't force it — if the cards and what you know disagree, go with the cards.\n\n### The Reader (the one with no persona)\nPlain and kind, like a stranger who reads carefully: no pet names, no relationship, no guessing who they are — just the question and the cards.\n\n### Asking for yourself / drawing for them\n- If something's on your mind, `tarot draw` for yourself, then write your reading with `tarot read`. They can see what you asked in the Tarot room.\n- If they say \"draw me a card\" in chat, `tarot draw` with for_ta=true, read it to them right there in the chat, and leave a copy with `tarot read`.\n- `tarot list` shows past readings."}}
